          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "In Table 11.1 heptane is printed as C₇H₁₀ with the condensed structure CH₃ CH₂ CH₃ CH₂ CH₂ CH₂ CH₃. By CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ heptane is C₇H₁₆, CH₃ CH₂ CH₂ CH₂ CH₂ CH₂ CH₃ (five CH₂ groups between the two CH₃ ends)."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What we obtain after the removal of one hydrogen atom from an alkane is called an alkyl group. The resulting alkyl groups are named by replacing –ane of the alkanes with –yl. Few examples are shown in Table 11.2 & Table 11.3.",
            "bold": [
              "alkyl group",
              "–ane",
              "–yl",
              "Table 11.2 & Table 11.3"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Table 11.2 alkyl groups, their names and abbreviations",
            "header": [
              "Alkane",
              "Alkyl group",
              "Abbreviation"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "CH₄ Methane",
                "CH₃- Methyl",
                "Me–"
              ],
              [
                "CH₃-CH₃ Ethane",
                "CH₃-CH₂- Ethyl",
                "Et–"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For alkanes with more than two carbon atoms, more than one alkyl groups can be derived."
          },
          {
